  "rent-increase-notice-co": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONSTRAINED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-18",
    lastChecked: "2026-10-01",
    topicKey: "rent-increase-notice",
    notes: "CO: CITED — C.R.S. § 38-12-701(2); § 38-12-702. 38-12-701(2)'s 60-day default notice applies only absent a written agreement; 38-12-702 (once-per-12-months cap) applies regardless and is restated. (Restored 2026-09-25 from lease-clause-citations-CO.csv, which still holds the same data.) | CO: SCRUB 2026-09-29 (checklist instruction 66, three-bucket test): kept the notice period the lease supplies; the restated once-a-year cap is covered by edu-rent-increase-frequency-co. | CO: CO retro (SOP 1.16) 2026-10-01, targeted fix 19 (rule 78 with rule 47). RESTORED a condition the 2026-09-29 scrub moved to education. C.R.S. § 38-12-702 caps rent increases at one per twelve-month period and applies regardless of what the parties agree, unlike § 38-12-701(2)'s 60-day default which applies only absent a written agreement. The trimmed clause left only a landlord-supplied notice period, so a landlord entering a lawful-looking \"30 days\" could comply with the clause and still breach § 38-12-702 by raising rent twice in a year - a frequency condition that a builder range check on a single notice-period field cannot catch, which is exactly what fix 19 says belongs back in the clause. Also widened from \"if this Lease continues on a month-to-month basis\" to any increase, because § 38-12-702 is not limited to periodic tenancies, and added the no-mid-term increase statement. Statute read section-open in the official 2026 C.R.S. (Colorado OLLS whole-title HTM, olls.info/crs/crs2026-title-38.htm / -13 / -24 / -06 / -00). Related: § 38-12-1307 bars a discriminatory, retaliatory or unconscionable increase used to circumvent part 13. Education: edu-rent-increase-frequency-co, edu-termination-notice-periods-co.",
  },
