    topicKey: "late-fee",
    notes: "NJ: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 from the official N.J.S.A. text supplied by Taylor (lis.njleg.state.nj.us), with per-section chapter-law history lines. Supersedes late-fee (base not tagged NJ). Controlling text: N.J.S.A. 2A:42-6.1 (amended 2001, c.142, s.1), 2A:42-6.3 (scope: seniors on SS Old Age/Railroad/other governmental pensions in lieu of SS, and SSDI, SSI, Work First NJ recipients); violation is a disorderly persons offense (2A:42-6.2). Also 2A:42-10.16a(a): after a warrant is posted/lockout, a tenant paying all rent within 3 business days cannot be charged a late fee above the amount in the warrant application. NOT LOCATED (bounded to text read): any general NJ late-fee cap or minimum grace period. NEEDS_REVIEW because the enforceability and eviction treatment of late fees in NJ is case law (whether a late fee is recoverable as 'rent' in a summary dispossess depends on lease wording and judicial doctrine) - not read. Builder: the senior/benefits sentence must stay in the clause because the landlord often cannot know a tenant's benefit status at signing; {{late_fee_grace_days}} alone could violate 2A:42-6.1 for covered tenants. | NJ: research pass 2026-09-26 (advanced research, report in conversation): Community Realty Mgmt. v. Harris, 155 N.J. 212 (1998): late and legal fees are recoverable as rent in summary dispossess only if the lease EXPRESSLY so provides; never against Section 8/public-housing tenants (Harris; Housing Auth. of Atlantic City v. Taylor, 171 N.J. 580 (2002); Hodges v. Sasil Corp., 189 N.J. 210 (2007)). 447 Associates v. Miranda, 115 N.J. 522 (1989): late fees not per se unreasonable; lease changes at renewal must be reasonable. No general statewide late-fee cap located (research 'not found'). Clause deliberately does NOT label the fee 'additional rent' pending Taylor's decision (NJ log s6). Stays NEEDS_REVIEW. | NJ: DECIDED 2026-09-26 (Taylor): late fees and legal fees are NOT labeled 'additional rent'. Effect under Harris: they are not recoverable as rent in a nonpayment summary dispossess and cannot by themselves support eviction - consistent with the library default in default-by-tenant ('failure to pay an assessed late fee ... will not by itself entitle Landlord to ... pursue eviction'). Collectible only as ordinary contract debt. NEEDS_REVIEW cleared; the unresolved question was the labeling choice, now decided. | NJ: SCRUB 2026-09-29 (checklist instruction 66, three-bucket test): kept the landlord's fee; the five-business-day grace period for senior and benefit-receiving tenants moved to edu-late-fee-rules-nj. | NJ: Retro check 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.12), rule 47. New Jersey penalizes offering or entering a lease with a provision that violates clearly established tenant rights ($100 per offense, N.J.S.A. 46:8-47; tenant may petition to end the lease, 46:8-48). After the 2026-09-29 scrub moved the senior grace rule to education, the clause let a landlord set a grace period shorter than five business days for a protected tenant, which N.J.S.A. 2A:42-6.1 forbids (scope 2A:42-6.3). The sentence restores the statutory floor inside the clause; it binds only where the statute does. edu-late-fee-rules-nj unchanged. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from the official NJ Legislature statutes (lis.njleg.state.nj.us, compilation through P.L.2026, c.30); text hashed, NJ retro sources.",
