  "fee-in-lieu-of-deposit-fl": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONDITIONAL"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-26",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-26",
    topicKey: "fee-in-lieu-of-deposit",
    notes: "FL: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov / leg.state.fl.us), with per-section history lines. Opt-in landlord right (instruction 30). Controlling text: Fla. Stat. §83.491 (s. 1, ch. 2023-181; s. 3, ch. 2024-199); applies to rental agreements entered into or renewed on or after 2023-07-01 (§83.491(9)). Written notice items: §83.491(3)(a)-(i), all carried in (a)-(g) plus the amount and refundability. Minimum agreement terms: §83.491(4)(a)1-5 (fee may not increase during the term; collection method and timing; the process and time frame to pay the deposit on default, with the credit-rating protection; termination at any time on paying the deposit; no adverse credit report once the deposit is paid). Capitalized disclosure: §83.491(4)(b) verbatim ('substantially the following form'). The agreement 'may not contain any clause that contradicts s. 83.45 or s. 83.47' (§83.491(4)(a)). Fee schedule: §83.491(5). End-of-tenancy notice and 15-day insurer wait: §83.491(2)(a)-(b); the claim must include an itemized list, supporting documents and any tenant objection; no double recovery from tenant and insurer (§83.491(2)(b)3); an insurer with subrogation rights has 1 year to seek reimbursement and the tenant keeps its defenses (§83.491(2)(b)2). Nondiscrimination and offer-to-all rule: §83.491(7). A fee, insurance product or surety bond accepted in lieu of a deposit is not a security deposit (§83.491(6)). STATUTE DEFECT recorded, not resolved: §83.491(6) cross-refers to the definition of security deposit as 's. 83.43(13)'; after the 2024 renumbering (s. 1, ch. 2024-199) that definition is §83.43(14), and (13) is 'rental agreement'. Layout (instruction 28): signed written agreement; capitalized disclosure. Builder: this row and security-deposit-return-fl both display for an FL lease that uses the fee option, because a deposit may still become due.",
  },
  "landlords-access-fl": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-26",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-26",
    topicKey: "landlord-entry",
    notes: "FL: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov / leg.state.fl.us), with per-section history lines. Supersedes landlords-access (base not tagged FL). Controlling text: Fla. Stat. §83.53 (last amended s. 3, ch. 2022-222). Why an override: the base grants a standing right to enter 'to show the property' on notice alone; §83.53(2) allows entry for the (1) purposes (inspection, showing, services) only with consent, in an emergency, when consent is unreasonably withheld, or after a half-period absence. The tenant's duty is not to withhold consent unreasonably (§83.53(1)). Repair entry: 'Reasonable notice' is at least 24 hours, 'reasonable time' is 7:30 a.m. to 8:00 p.m. (§83.53(2)). Protection or preservation: any time. Absence rule: §83.53(2)(d). Anti-harassment: §83.53(3). The final 24-hour sentence is voluntary and tenant-favourable, keeping the library's 24-hour default. Whether a lease can give advance blanket consent to showings is untested here; this row avoids relying on it. Remedies for abuse: §83.55 damages; §83.64 retaliation. | FL research pass 2026-09-26 (advanced research, once; report in the conversation). No Florida case on advance blanket consent to entry found; the research analysis (not authority) is that consent on notice at reasonable times tracking §83.53 is likely enforceable, and entry without notice or at any hour risks voidness under §83.47. The row tracks §83.53 directly, so it does not depend on the question.",
  },
