    topicKey: "owner-identity-disclosure",
    notes: "FL: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov / leg.state.fl.us), with per-section history lines. Controlling text: Fla. Stat. §83.50 (s. 3, ch. 2025-16 latest): in addition to any other required disclosure, the landlord or a person authorized to enter into the rental agreement for the landlord must disclose in writing, at or before commencement of the tenancy, the name and address of the landlord or a person authorized to receive notices and demands. The authorized person keeps authority until the tenant is notified otherwise; notices of names and addresses go to the residence or a tenant-specified address, or by §83.505 e-mail. Why REQUIRED here: the lease is the natural at-or-before-commencement writing. No sanction stated in §83.50; §83.55 damages; and §83.60(1)(b) lets a tenant's 7-day notice go to the §83.50 designee. No Florida registration or disclosure-as-eviction-precondition rule in Part II (contrast NJ 46:8-33).",
