    lastChecked: "2026-09-27",
    topicKey: "pet-policy",
    notes: "AZ: Read section-open 2026-09-26/27 in the browser from azleg.gov (official legislative compilation, stated as updated through the 57th Legislature, 2nd Regular Session, 2026). azleg ARS pages print no history lines, so currency rests on the compilation date and the session-law check in the AZ log (s1.1). Why the base fails: pet-policy lets Landlord enter and remove a pet 'without liability' on grounds (illness, suspected neglect) outside A.R.S. §33-1343's emergency and notice rules and §33-1343(E)'s closed list of other access; 'without liability' limits liability arising under law (§33-1315(A)(3), with the §33-1315(B) penalty); and Arizona has its own death-or-incapacity pet procedure (§33-1314(E)-(G), §33-1370(E)). The base's tenant indemnity is dropped (as NV did) because it could reach the landlord's own statutory liability (§33-1315(A)(3) bars an agreement to indemnify the landlord for that liability); the damage-responsibility sentence remains. Deposit: §33-1321(A) security however denominated (cap), §33-1321(B) nonrefundable only if purpose stated in writing. Abandoned animals: §33-1370(E) (release to shelter after one calendar day if the authorized person does not retrieve; otherwise reasonable care; good-faith immunity).",
