    topicKey: "abandoned-property",
    notes: "TN: TN-SCOPE: STATEWIDE TEXT; the statute binds Act counties, and elsewhere the clause adopts the Act procedure by contract as the safest course. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from Justia's host copy of the 2025 Tennessee Code (the official code is published by LexisNexis), with each section's history line; currency checked against the full text of every public chapter of the 114th General Assembly (2025 and 2026 Pub. Ch. 1 to 1142 and the seven 2025 First Extraordinary Session acts, publications.tnsosfiles.com) and against the LexisNexis Tennessee Code Unannotated free public access site, which already prints the 2026 Pub. Ch. 606 sections (TN log §1). Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-405 (Acts 2005, ch. 156, § 5): (a) 30 days' unexplained absence without rent is prima facie abandonment, reentry authorised; (b)(1) 15 days' nonpayment plus circumstances; (b)(2) posting AND regular mail to the premises address with the four statements; (b)(3) phone and mailing address; (b)(4) contact within 10 days and intent to stay -> judicial process; (c) store at least 30 days, then sell or dispose, proceeds to rent, damages, storage, sale costs, attorney's fees, balance held six months. Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-507(c): reasonable efforts to rerent; lease ends when a new tenancy begins. OTHER COUNTIES: no abandonment or left-property statute (LexisNexis 'abandon! w/25 (tenant or lessee or occupant) w/25 (property or possessions or belongings)': 5 hits, all in Chapter 28; TN log §17). After a DETAINER JUDGMENT the separate statewide rule applies instead: property is set out and may be discarded after 48 hours (Tenn. Code Ann. § 29-18-127(b); edu-eviction-process-tn). RF421 § 18 adds that the six-month balance then becomes the landlord's - the statute does not say so, and this clause does not claim it (TN log §15). COUNTY-LAYER PILOT (Taylor, 2026-09-28): Tennessee is the test case for the future county/local layer. The URLTA (Tenn. Code Ann. Title 66, Chapter 28) applies only in the 17 counties over 75,000 people in the 2010 census, a list frozen by 2021 Pub. Ch. 182 § 2 (Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-102(a)): Anderson, Blount, Bradley, Davidson, Hamilton, Knox, Madison, Maury, Montgomery, Rutherford, Sevier, Shelby, Sullivan, Sumner, Washington, Williamson and Wilson.",
