    notes: "TN: TN-SCOPE: STATEWIDE TEXT; statutory in Act counties, contractual elsewhere. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from Justia's host copy of the 2025 Tennessee Code (the official code is published by LexisNexis), with each section's history line; currency checked against the full text of every public chapter of the 114th General Assembly (2025 and 2026 Pub. Ch. 1 to 1142 and the seven 2025 First Extraordinary Session acts, publications.tnsosfiles.com) and against the LexisNexis Tennessee Code Unannotated free public access site, which already prints the 2026 Pub. Ch. 606 sections (TN log §1). OPT-IN LANDLORD RIGHT (instruction 30). Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-304(c): landlord and tenant 'may agree in writing that the tenant perform specified repairs, maintenance tasks, alterations, and remodeling, but only if the transaction is entered into in good faith and not for the purpose of evading the obligations of the landlord'; (d) the landlord may not treat performance of 'the separate agreement' as a condition of any obligation under the rental agreement. Unlike SC (single-family carve-out) Tennessee has no dwelling-type distinction. RF421 § 4 allocates fence, driveway, walls, flooring, pool, fixtures, walkways, patio, landscaping and outbuildings by checkbox (TN log §15). Outside Act counties there is no statutory repair duty to evade (common-law habitability is case law, instruction 16). COUNTY-LAYER PILOT (Taylor, 2026-09-28): Tennessee is the test case for the future county/local layer. The URLTA (Tenn. Code Ann. Title 66, Chapter 28) applies only in the 17 counties over 75,000 people in the 2010 census, a list frozen by 2021 Pub. Ch. 182 § 2 (Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-102(a)): Anderson, Blount, Bradley, Davidson, Hamilton, Knox, Madison, Maury, Montgomery, Rutherford, Sevier, Shelby, Sullivan, Sumner, Washington, Williamson and Wilson. | Retro check 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.4), rule 48 (separate documents): Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-304(c) lets the parties 'agree in writing' that the tenant perform specified repairs, and (d) calls it 'the separate agreement described in subsection (c)' that may not be a condition of 'any rental agreement' (read section-open 2026-09-28 (Justia host copy with history line; TN log §1); the only residential hit in the 2026-09-30 search for separate-agreement wording). The earlier text sat inside the Lease and only declared itself separate. Now a separate signed writing, following the AZ and AL pattern (maintenance-allocation-az, tenant-repair-agreement-al). LAYOUT (Addendum M.12): SEPARATE-DOCUMENT output needed; the bracketed instruction flags it. Outside Act counties § 66-28-304 does not apply, but the separate form is lawful everywhere, so one text is kept.",
