    notes: "VA: VA-SCOPE: 4 OR FEWER VIRGINIA UNITS ONLY (pair alternative; use only where the landlord owns four or fewer rental dwelling units in Virginia, or up to a 10 percent interest in four or fewer, counting units held individually or through a business entity). Pair with acceptable-payment-methods-va, choice group va-size-payment-methods. OPT-IN landlord right (instruction 30): the card refusal is lawful only for this landlord. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser on the official Code of Virginia (law.lis.virginia.gov; Title 55.1 Chapter 12 read whole from the vacodefull page, which prints the 2026-session amendments and every delayed-effective version with its history line); 2024-2026 amending chapters read in enrolled text on lis.virginia.gov with their effective-date clauses (VA log §1). Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1204(J) (2025 Acts ch. 627 and ch. 655; 2026 Acts ch. 722 and ch. 723, effective 2026-07-01): (J)(1) the landlord 'shall accept payment of periodic rent and any security deposit by check and money order'; written receipt 'whenever the tenant pays rent in the form of cash or money order' (the 'upon request' limit was struck in 2026); no fee for collecting or processing any payment 'unless the landlord offers an alternative method of payment that does not include additional fees'; any such fee capped at the actual out-of-pocket third-party cost for card or electronic payment; (J)(2) a landlord with four or fewer rental dwelling units, or up to a 10 percent interest in four or fewer, 'shall not be required to accept payment of periodic rent and any security deposit by debit or credit card'. Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1204(I): no unilateral change in lease terms without notice AND both parties' written consent, which is why the shared acceptable-payment-methods row ('Landlord may change the accepted payment methods on reasonable written notice') was not tagged. OPEN QUESTION, recorded not resolved: (J)(2) exempts small landlords from accepting cards, but no provision read affirmatively requires a landlord with more than four units to accept cards; the default variant therefore neither promises nor refuses cards and leaves the list to the landlord. SIZE-TIER PILOT (Taylor, 2026-09-28, VA decision 1): Virginia's Act covers every residential landlord (the old two-single-family-home opt-out was dropped in the 2019 recodification), but several rules turn on how many Virginia rental units the landlord owns: 'more than four rental dwelling units or more than a 10 percent interest in more than four rental dwelling units, whether individually or through a business entity, in the Commonwealth'. Four pairs (choice groups va-size-*) carry the lease wording that differs; the more-than-four variant is the default because it is lawful for every landlord. Product flag: a per-state 'Virginia rental units owned' landlord attribute (VA log §14).",
