  "homestead-waiver-va": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONDITIONAL"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-28",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "homestead-waiver",
    notes: "VA: VA-SCOPE: STATEWIDE. OPTIONAL CLAUSE CHOSEN BY TAYLOR (VA decision 2, 2026-09-28; recommendation was education only). Outside-title sections read section-open 2026-09-28 from the official Code of Virginia Law Library compilation (law.lis.virginia.gov/law-library, per-title CSV) overlaid with every section on the official 2026 Updates pages (VA log §17). Va. Code Ann. § 34-22: a person who declares 'in a bond, bill, note or other instrument by which he is or may become liable for the payment of money' that he waives the homestead exemption subjects the property to that obligation, but the waiver 'shall not extend to or affect' the exemptions under §§ 34-26 (poor debtor's exempt articles), 34-27 (agricultural articles) and 34-29 (wage garnishment limits); the first sentence of this clause is the statute's own sufficient wording. Va. Code Ann. § 34-4 (householder exemption: $5,000, or $10,000 if 65 or older, plus up to $50,000 in a principal residence and $500 per dependent; CPI adjustment from 2027-04-01); Va. Code Ann. § 34-25 (judgment on an instrument waiving the homestead must say so; the plaintiff must plead it). 2026 additions not affected by any waiver on their terms: § 34-4.3 ($1,000 minimum account balance exempt from garnishment) and § 34-4.4 (benefit payments) - read by opening text only. RISKS (recorded, not resolved): (1) it matters only in collection after a money judgment; (2) the FTC Credit Practices Rule (16 C.F.R. § 444.2, FEDERAL, not read) bars exemption waivers in consumer credit contracts by lenders and retail installment sellers - whether a residential lease is within it was not researched; (3) Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1208(A)(1) bars waiving rights 'under this chapter' only, and the homestead exemption is Title 34, so the waiver is not a prohibited lease provision on the text read; (4) tenant-facing tone. The Virginia REALTORS Form 200 (Rev. 07/23) § 36 contains a homestead waiver.",
  },
  "security-deposit-return-al": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-28",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-29",
    topicKey: "security-deposit-return",
    notes: "AL: Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from the official Code of Alabama 1975 on ALISON (alison.legislature.state.al.us, the Legislature's own code service; Title 35 Chapter 9A read whole, all 48 section numbers, with every history line; newest Chapter 9A amendment Act 2018-473; whole Code loaded, 49,638 section versions including versions effective 2026-10-01 and later; every 2024, 2025 and 2026 act touching landlord-tenant terms screened; AL log §1). Instruction 24 family closed for AL: supersedes the blank-states parent `security-deposit-return` (M.11). Controlling text: Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(b) (at termination, security may be applied to accrued rent and damages from the tenant's noncompliance with Ala. Code § 35-9A-301, 'all as itemized by the landlord in a written notice delivered to the tenant together with the amount due 60 days after termination of the tenancy and delivery of possession'); Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(c) (itemized list of amounts withheld within the 60-day period); Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(d) (tenant gives a valid forwarding address in writing on vacating; otherwise first-class mail to the last known address or, if none, the property address; 'Any deposit unclaimed by the tenant as well as any check outstanding shall be forfeited by the tenant after a period of 90 days'); Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(e) (first-class mailing to the tenant's written address within 60 days is sufficient compliance); Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(f) (late refund or accounting: landlord pays DOUBLE THE ORIGINAL DEPOSIT); Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(g) (other damages preserved); Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(h) (holder of the landlord's interest at termination bound). History: Act 2006-316; Act 2014-279 (the 2014 act lengthened the period to 60 days and added the forwarding-address and 90-day forfeiture rules; the 2006-era 35-day forms are stale, AL log §15). Seller stays liable for security and prepaid rent (Ala. Code § 35-9A-205(a)). 'Ordinary wear and tear' is not in the statute; it follows from limiting damages to Ala. Code § 35-9A-301 noncompliance (conservative contract wording). Casualty, ouster, landlord-noncompliance and possession-failure terminations require return of recoverable security and unearned prepaid rent (Ala. Code § 35-9A-401(d); Ala. Code § 35-9A-402(a)(1); Ala. Code § 35-9A-404(b)(1); Ala. Code § 35-9A-406(b); Ala. Code § 35-9A-407). The 90-day forfeiture interacts with the Uniform Unclaimed Property Act (Ala. Code § 35-12-72) in a way no statute resolves; recorded in `edu-security-deposit-rules-al`, not relied on. Deposit waivers are prohibited (Ala. Code § 35-9A-163(a)(1)). | AL: SCRUB 2026-09-29 (checklist instruction 66, three-bucket test): kept the landlord's application right, the tenant's forwarding-address duty and the 90-day forfeiture; the restated 60-day refund, wear-and-tear, mailing and sale rules are in edu-security-deposit-rules-al.",
  },
  "security-deposit-cap-al": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONSTRAINED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-28",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-29",
    topicKey: "security-deposit-cap",
    notes: "AL: Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from the official Code of Alabama 1975 on ALISON (alison.legislature.state.al.us, the Legislature's own code service; Title 35 Chapter 9A read whole, all 48 section numbers, with every history line; newest Chapter 9A amendment Act 2018-473; whole Code loaded, 49,638 section versions including versions effective 2026-10-01 and later; every 2024, 2025 and 2026 act touching landlord-tenant terms screened; AL log §1). Controlling text: Ala. Code § 35-9A-201(a) ('A landlord may not demand or receive money as security, in an amount in excess of one month's periodic rent, except for pets, changes to the premises, or increased liability risks to the landlord or premises, for tenant's obligations under a rental agreement'). The statute sets no cap on the excepted categories and does not say how 'increased liability risks' is measured; the clause asks the landlord to name the purpose so the exception is visible (conservative drafting). Prepaid rent: the section title is 'Security Deposits; Prepaid Rent' and the cap reaches only 'money as security'; whether advance last-month rent is security is not settled by the text (GA took the stricter view for Georgia; for Alabama the clause states the prepaid-rent reading only for rent tied to a specific period). No penalty specific to the cap located; Ala. Code § 35-9A-163(a)(1) bars waiving deposit requirements. Compare `security-deposit-cap-az` (1.5 months) and `security-deposit-cap-ga` (2 months). | AL: SCRUB 2026-09-29 (checklist instruction 66, three-bucket test): kept the landlord's additional-security terms; the restated one-month cap is in edu-security-deposit-rules-al. Basis recorded as the landlord's own term rather than a required disclosure, since whether § 35-9A-201(a) requires the statement in the lease was never confirmed.",
  },
