    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-29",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-29",
    topicKey: "abandoned-property",
    notes: "UT: Utah Code Ann. § 78B-6-815(1)-(2) (presumption; declaration of abandonment with prescribed language, served under Utah Code Ann. § 78B-6-805; 24 hours excluding weekends and court holidays) and Utah Code Ann. § 78B-6-816(2)-(11) (notice by posting and first-class mail; 15 calendar days; costs; up-to-15-day extension; public sale with 5 days' mailed notice or charitable donation; no storage of listed items, (6), (8); surplus under Title 67, Chapter 4a; motor vehicles excluded, (4)). Post-eviction property: removal and storage by the sheriff or constable, who may delegate to the landlord (Utah Code Ann. § 78B-6-812(4)(b)); access within 5 business days to clothing, identification, financial, public-benefits and medical items without paying costs ((4)(c)); then abandoned property under Utah Code Ann. § 78B-6-816 ((4)(d)); a pet goes to the tenant if present, otherwise to animal control ((4)(e)-(f)); version effective 2026-09-01 (Laws of Utah 2026, ch. 44). Tenant-present sale limits: Utah Code Ann. § 78B-6-816(10); 'verified medical provider': (7)(b). The statute speaks of abandonment and eviction; applying the same procedure to property left after an ordinary end-of-term move-out is contractual and at least as protective as silence. Sits under surrender-end-of-term-ks-ne. No Utah rule lets the lease displace the statute. Read section-open 2026-09-29 in the built-in browser from the Legislature's official Utah Code XML on le.utah.gov ('Current Version' files, which print each section's history line; effective dates taken from each section's version id): Title 57 Chapters 17, 20, 21, 22, 24 and 27 and Title 78B Chapter 6 Parts 8 and 8a read whole; Chapter 78B-6a (Laws of Utah 2026, ch. 401) read in the parts cited; the whole Utah Code (96 titles, 28,551 section versions) and the Utah Constitution (23 articles) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits (UT log s1, s17).",
