    topicKey: "criminal-activity",
    notes: "IL: 735 ILCS 5/9-120(a) (P.A. 100-173): 'A written lease shall notify the lessee that if any lessee or occupant, on one or more occasions, uses or permits the use of the leased premises for the commission of a felony or Class A misdemeanor under the laws of this State, the lessor shall have the right to void the lease and recover the leased premises.' The clause follows the statutory sentence with only these changes: 'the lessor' becomes 'Landlord', 'the lease' becomes 'this Lease', and 'this State' becomes 'the State of Illinois' (rule 59). Omission does not waive the lessor's right (same subsection), so the clause is required text rather than a condition of the right. The procedure (5 days' written notice to vacate after voiding, (d); 7-day stay limit, (f); deposit not forfeited except sheriff fees, (c)) is in edu-expedited-criminal-eviction-il. Controls over any cure promise in default-by-tenant: the statute voids the lease at the lessor's option without a cure (Step D rule 43, IL log §19). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
