    topicKey: "returned-payments",
    notes: "IL: Supersedes returned-payments for IL: its 'not to exceed the maximum amount permitted by applicable law' states only a ceiling (rule 53), and 765 ILCS 705/35(b) makes the fee unrecoverable unless stated on the lease's first page, so it must be a stated figure (carried into fee-disclosure-first-page-il). 810 ILCS 5/3-806: a person who issues a check dishonored for no account, insufficient funds or insufficient credit is liable for $25 or all costs and expenses of collection including reasonable attorney's fees, whichever is greater, plus interest under 815 ILCS 205/4(1); costs above $25 in a nonlitigated collection need a written demand by certified mail, return receipt requested, demanding payment within 30 days and stating the liability. The section does not reach stop-payment orders or failed electronic transfers, which are contract only. Whether a lease may set a higher fixed fee for a dishonored check is not settled by the statute (not read in case law). The last clause preserves 765 ILCS 705/4 (no required electronic payment) and 765 ILCS 705/3.5 (portal fee alternative); a demand for certified funds is not an electronic transfer. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
