    topicKey: "pet-policy",
    notes: "IL: Supersedes pet-policy for IL: the base clause's indemnity 'from claims arising from Tenant's pet(s)' and entry and removal 'without liability to Tenant' can exempt the landlord from liability for its own negligence, which 765 ILCS 705/1(a) makes void for residential leases ('every covenant ... exempting the lessor from liability for damages for injuries to person or property caused by or resulting from the negligence of the lessor, his or her agents, servants or employees ... shall be deemed to be void'; (b) saves only non-residential property-damage waivers). Wording follows pet-policy-pa without its Pennsylvania deposit-cap sentence (Illinois has no deposit cap). A pet deposit secures damage, so it is a security deposit under 765 ILCS 710/1(a). Pet rent is a recurring non-optional fee once a pet is approved, listed on page 1 (fee-disclosure-first-page-il). Assistance animals: no pet deposit, fee or special insurance (310 ILCS 120/10(f)); covered by assistance-animal-accommodation. No Illinois statute on pets in house or apartment leases (whole-code battery 24: only 765 ILCS 745/9, mobile homes). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
