    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-30",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-30",
    topicKey: "owner-identity-disclosure",
    notes: "IL: 735 ILCS 5/9-320(a) (P.A. 83-707): an owner of residential real property containing more than 4 living units who does not reside or maintain an office there and does not employ a resident or on-site manager or agent must post, near the mailboxes or inside in a place visible to all residents, a notice of at least 20 square inches with the name, address and telephone number of the person responsible for managing the building and of the company or companies insuring it against fire or explosion (or that it is uninsured), and post notice of cancellation within 24 hours of being notified. (b): 'In lieu of the requirement for posting the notices ... the owner's managing agent may include such notice in a written rental or lease agreement or may give such notice by first class mail'. (c): petty offense, fine up to $100 per day. The lease is one permitted vehicle for a required notice, so the basis is a required disclosure. Illinois has no general statewide owner-identity disclosure for other landlords (whole-code; local ordinances such as Chicago's not read). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
