    topicKey: "utility-apportionment",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 740/5(a) (P.A. 87-176; 88-417): no landlord may demand payment for master metered public utility services under a lease provision for a proportionate share 'without the landlord first providing the tenant with a copy in writing either as part of the lease or another written agreement of the formula used'; total payments may not exceed the utility's bill; the formula must include all users and may reflect apartment size or usage; the utility bill must be made available on request. A lease that includes utilities in rent without allocating them is unaffected (same subsection). Any utility charge Tenant must pay is a non-optional fee listed on page 1 (fee-disclosure-first-page-il). Tenant-paid meters that also serve common areas or other units: 765 ILCS 735/1.2 requires a separate written statement and 12 months of bills before a lease is offered, and bars switching from landlord-paid to tenant-paid utilities during a lease term (edu-shared-meter-utilities-il). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
