    lastChecked: "2026-09-30",
    topicKey: "landlord-self-cure",
    notes: "TN: TN-SCOPE: NON-ACT COUNTIES (optional). In the 17 URLTA counties the right exists by statute whether or not the lease mentions it (Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-506), so the clause is unnecessary there; its text tracks § 66-28-506 so it is harmless if used. Retro check 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.4), rule 27 (landlord-self-cure) and rule 54 (optional clause). TAYLOR'S DECISION (2026-09-30): offer it as an optional clause for properties outside the 17 counties. Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-506 (Acts 1975, ch. 245, § 4.202), read section-open 2026-09-28 (Justia host copy with history line; TN log §1): tenant noncompliance with § 66-28-401 materially affecting health and safety that can be remedied by repair, replacement of a damaged item or cleaning; tenant fails to comply as promptly as conditions require in an emergency or within 14 days after written notice; landlord may enter, cause the work to be done in a workmanlike manner and submit an itemized bill for the actual and reasonable cost or fair and reasonable value as rent on the next periodic rent date, or for immediate payment if the rental agreement has terminated. Outside Act counties no statute gives or bars the right; it is contractual. Entry in the other counties follows landlords-access-tn-other. BUILDER / BACKLOG (Taylor, 2026-09-30): link to the property-county attribute backlog item (TN log §14, Addendum M): show this clause only when the property is outside the 17 URLTA counties. COUNTY-LAYER PILOT (Taylor, 2026-09-28): Tennessee is the test case for the future county/local layer. The URLTA (Tenn. Code Ann. Title 66, Chapter 28) applies only in the 17 counties over 75,000 people in the 2010 census, a list frozen by 2021 Pub. Ch. 182 § 2 (Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-102(a)): Anderson, Blount, Bradley, Davidson, Hamilton, Knox, Madison, Maury, Montgomery, Rutherford, Sevier, Shelby, Sullivan, Sumner, Washington, Williamson and Wilson.",
