    notes: "SC: SC retro 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.9, rule 54t). OPTIONAL clause (CONDITIONAL, not default), state version of `tenant-caused-damage-tn` (read; TN row not tagged). TAYLOR'S DECISION (2026-09-30): offer the clause plus `edu-tenant-caused-damage-sc`, after being told that `casualty-landlord-termination-sc` does not cover lost rent. Only TN's part (2) is used: part (1) (no abatement while the lease continues) would waive the rent-reduction right in S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-650(a)(2), which has no fault exception, and a lease may not waive chapter rights (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-330(a)(1)), with a penalty for knowingly using such a term (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-330(b)). Part (2) states the measure of the actual damages the landlord may already recover for the tenant's breach of S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-510(6) (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-710(C), S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-750, S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-410(d)), with the duty to mitigate (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-50(a)) and the periodic-tenancy limit modelled on S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-730(c) and S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-770(a)-(b) (rule 37). The last sentence keeps the tenant's statutory casualty rights. UNREAD RISK (rule 76 label): no South Carolina case law was read on whether a court would award lost rent after a tenant ends the lease under S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-650(a)(1), or treat the clause as burdening that statutory exit; occupants and guests go beyond the statute's 'knowingly permit' standard in S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-510(6) as a contract term. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from the official South Carolina Code of Laws on scstatehouse.gov (the site states the Code is current through the 2025 session; whole Code reloaded, 1,304 chapter pages, control term 0; 2026 Act Nos. 184 and 252 read from the ratified acts; section text saved and SHA-256 matched to the browser, SC retro sources).",
