  "criminal-activity-mo": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONDITIONAL"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-30",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-30",
    topicKey: "criminal-activity",
    notes: "MO: Optional clause (rule 54; crime-free / criminal-activity family, model criminal-activity-nc). Missouri law: a tenant may not 'violate any of the conditions of his written lease' (Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.030), and on a violation the landlord may re-enter 'after giving ten days' notice to vacate the premises ... by the procedure specified by law' (Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.040); no cure right is stated. Allowing illegal possession, sale or distribution of controlled substances on the premises makes the lease void (Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.020); drug-related criminal activity also supports the expedited eviction track (Mo. Rev. Stat. §§ 441.710 to 441.880; edu-expedited-drug-eviction-mo). The clause adds non-drug criminal activity that threatens safety, which no statute makes a ground, as a lease condition. Carve-outs: (1) Mo. Const. art. XIV, § 2.3(4) bars a post-12/8/2022 lease from prohibiting lawful possession and non-smoking consumption of marijuana (edu-cannabis-mo); (2) Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.920(2): no tenant may be evicted or found in violation of a lease because the tenant is, has been or is in imminent danger of becoming a victim of domestic violence, sexual assault or stalking (subject to the section's exceptions; edu-dv-protections-mo). Whether a court treats a given lease term as a 'condition' whose breach supports Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.040 re-entry is case law, not read. No Missouri statute addresses crime-free addenda (MO battery 99: 0 hits). Not a default. Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from revisor.mo.gov (Missouri Revisor of Statutes, which prints each section with its history line and version date and describes its text as unofficial and uncertified): Chapters 441, 534 and 535 read whole (72, 45 and 23 sections as indexed); the whole Revised Statutes of Missouri and the Missouri Constitution (30,836 entries) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits (MO log §1, MO log §17).",
  },
  "dv-termination-fee-mo": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONDITIONAL"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-30",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-30",
    topicKey: "dv-lease-termination",
    notes: "MO: Optional clause (rule 54). Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.920(6): 'Any landlord or property owner may impose a reasonable termination fee on a tenant or lessee who desires to terminate a lease before the expiration date of such lease under the provisions of this section.' Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.920(3) gives the tenant an affirmative defense to rent for the period after the tenant vacates if the court finds the tenant was a victim or in imminent danger and the tenant notified the landlord and provided any requested documentation (Mo. Rev. Stat. § 441.920(4)). The bracket states the landlord's figure (rule 55); the statute requires only that it be 'reasonable' (no figure; case law not read), so the builder should prompt for a modest amount (MO log §10). The clause displaces the early-termination fee in early-termination-ks for this exit. Not a default. See edu-dv-protections-mo. Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from revisor.mo.gov (Missouri Revisor of Statutes, which prints each section with its history line and version date and describes its text as unofficial and uncertified): Chapters 441, 534 and 535 read whole (72, 45 and 23 sections as indexed); the whole Revised Statutes of Missouri and the Missouri Constitution (30,836 entries) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits (MO log §1, MO log §17).",
  },
