    topicKey: "holdover-rate",
    notes: "IN: Optional clause (rule 54; standing GA decision: offer an opt-in holdover rate where no statutory measure exists). No Indiana holdover statute or double-rent measure (IN battery 34: 19 sections, none a residential holdover rule; known-positive test passed); a general tenancy by the landlord's consent is month to month (Ind. Code § 32-31-1-2), and notice is not required to terminate the lease of a tenant at sufferance (Ind. Code § 32-31-1-8(4)); whether a tenant holding over without consent is one is case law, not read. Text is holdover-rate-ga's, an add-on to holdover-ca (tagged IN). Whether a given rate is liquidated damages or a penalty is Indiana case law, not read; the agreed-estimate sentence is common drafting, not a safe harbour. Whether accepting money after the Term creates a month-to-month tenancy despite the acceptance sentence is also case law, not read. BUILDER: same as holdover-rate-ga (show only on opt-in; place after holdover-ca; require {{holdover_daily_rate}}; show the daily equivalent of Monthly Rent). Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
